Subject: Register analysis tables start zeroed instead of holding allocator garbage

What this does
  - `regvm_own_analyze` sets `init_set` and `undef_set` to `REG_INIT_UNINIT` right after their allocation succeeds.
  - `js_regvm_encode` zeroes the per-slot definition counters `bcnt` before the first block is bucketed.

Why we need it
  Both arrays came from `js_malloc` and were read before every entry had been written: `init_set`/`undef_set` through the "needs a fill" test and `bcnt` through the per-block counters. Which registers were marked, and how the definitions were bucketed, depended on what the allocator happened to return.

Failure
  Valgrind reported conditional jumps on uninitialised values in these two functions for ordinary register-build programs, which also masked every other finding in a valgrind sweep of the kit cases. The effect on code was a register spuriously treated as needing a fill (a wasted store) or, for `bcnt`, a mis-sized bucket.

Soundness
  - The all-clear values are the ones the code already assumes ("only registers proved to need a fill are marked"; "counters start at zero").
  - Registers that were marked by the garbage and are not marked now are ones the analysis never proved; every proved one is still marked by the existing code.
  - Allocation size and lifetime are unchanged, and no stack-build code is touched.
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -75384,0 +75385,3 @@
+    /* Both sets start empty: only registers proved to need a fill are marked. */
+    memset(init_set, REG_INIT_UNINIT, nreg);
+    memset(undef_set, REG_INIT_UNINIT, nreg);
@@ -76624,0 +76628,3 @@
+            /* The per-slot definition counters start at zero; only the touched
+               entries are reset after each block. */
+            memset(bcnt, 0, (size_t)(fd->var_count + 1) * sizeof(int));
